{"id": "prado-Q18222990", "status": "ok", "meta": "The Blind Sculptor, signed and dated by Jusepe de Ribera in 1632, may be an Allegory of Touch; it has been in the Prado since 1837.", "about": "A blind man is the subject of this painting, signed and dated by Jusepe de Ribera in 1632. For years it was called The Blind Man of Gambazzo, after the sculptor Giovanni Gonnelli. That idea was dropped because Gonnelli was only thirty then and the sitter is clearly older. Another suggestion is the philosopher Carneades, who could recognize the god Pan by touching the head of a statue. Wikipedia says the most likely reading is Allegory of Touch, from a series on the five senses. It reached the Prado in 1837.", "museum_record": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ARibera-escultor_ciego.jpg", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3ARibera-escultor_ciego.jpg", "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q18222990", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Blind_Sculptor"], "check": "clean"}
{"id": "prado-Q2304870", "status": "ok", "meta": "Seven sins in a ring around four last things: The Seven Deadly Sins and the Four Last Things, attributed to Hieronymus Bosch or a follower.", "about": "Seven sins fill a large circle, ringed by four small circles for the four last things, among them death and judgment. Wrath sits at the bottom, and clockwise from there come envy, greed, gluttony, sloth, extravagance and pride, each drawn from ordinary life instead of allegory. The painting is attributed to Hieronymus Bosch or to a follower. In 2015 the Bosch Research and Conservation Project called it the work of a follower, but scholars at the Prado dismissed that. It hangs there in a sealed case.", "museum_record": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHieronymus_Bosch-_The_Seven_Deadly_Sins_and_the_Four_Last_Things.JPG", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AHieronymus_Bosch-_The_Seven_Deadly_Sins_and_the_Four_Last_Things.JPG", "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q2304870", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Seven_Deadly_Sins_and_the_Four_Last_Things"], "check": "clean"}
