{"id": "prado-Q27749021", "status": "ok", "meta": "The Virgin appears to Saint Bernard as he studies, in Bartolomé Esteban Murillo's painting of about 1655 in the Prado.", "about": "Saint Bernard of Clairvaux falls to his knees as the Virgin appears to him while he studies. His books and desk are in the background. Bartolomé Esteban Murillo painted it around 1655, and Wikipedia calls it one of his most important tenebrist works. It was paired with The Apparition of the Virgin to Saint Ildefonsus. Both were probably made as altarpieces for a Cistercian convent, though nothing records where they came from, and art historians disagree on the date of this one. Some put it before 1655. Others say it could be as late as 1670.", "museum_record": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AAparici%C3%B3n_de_la_Virgen_a_san_Bernardo.jpg", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AAparici%C3%B3n_de_la_Virgen_a_san_Bernardo.jpg", "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q27749021", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Apparition_of_the_Virgin_to_Saint_Bernard_(Murillo)"], "check": "clean"}
