{"id": "rcol-Q28016106", "status": "ok", "meta": "A partly undressed woman pulls on a stocking in Jan Steen's Woman at her Toilet of 1663, which hangs in Buckingham Palace.", "about": "A partly undressed woman sits on her bed, pulling on a stocking, and looks at us with a flirtatious gaze. An arched doorway splits the picture in two. Wikipedia reads the foreground as moral, with a sunflower for constancy, grapevines for domestic virtue and a weeping cherub for chastised profane love. Beyond the arch lie a skull, an extinguished candle and a lute with a broken string, for vanity. Jan Steen painted it in 1663. King George IV acquired it in 1821.", "museum_record": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_Woman_at_her_Toilet%2C_by_Jan_Steen.jpg", "inventory_number": "", "dimensions": "", "credit_line": "", "sources": ["https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File%3AA_Woman_at_her_Toilet%2C_by_Jan_Steen.jpg", "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q28016106", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Woman_at_her_Toilet_(Steen)"], "check": "clean"}
